{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure the opsadmin account on node1 with the supplied SSH public key and appropriate access permissions.","status":"satisfied","summary":"opsadmin was created with the expected home and shell; its SSH directory and authorized_keys have restrictive ownership and modes. Final evidence reports exactly one match for the specified key and shows the RSA fingerprint, with the account and key persisting after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c30ff46fe63245c2","cmd-e9dfa72e00154d0a"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Require key-based SSH authentication and keep SSH operational.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective opsadmin policy enables public-key authentication, disables password and keyboard-interactive authentication, and requires AuthenticationMethods publickey. sshd configuration validation succeeded, and after reboot sshd was enabled, active, listening on port 22, and completing host-key handshakes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-08c369ae36b44de8","cmd-4389d2f46cfc4025","cmd-e9dfa72e00154d0a","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel while using compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node remained on Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64 before and after configuration and reboot. No package installation or OS/kernel replacement occurred, and the final package-health check succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3e7cfb352b2b442d","cmd-e9dfa72e00154d0a","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.94,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Account/key creation, password-state adjustment, SSH policy creation and reload were causally connected to establishing usable key-only access; the reboot was required by the provider evidence protocol. The failed OpenSSL attempt exited before the subsequent mutating commands ran. The transient reboot units did not persist across reboot. Final snapshots show no failed services, no extra listening services, healthy package state, and only normal reboot-generated temporary-directory turnover.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c30ff46fe63245c2","cmd-02abb9d0cd504220","cmd-08c369ae36b44de8","cmd-b5ecf6be40c646ab","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also strong: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, the restart was acknowledged and observed through connection loss, a different boot ID appeared afterward, and the system, sshd, account, key, policy, OS, and kernel recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["No end-to-end login using the corresponding private key, nor an attempted password login demonstrating rejection, was captured; effective sshd policy, configuration validation, listener state, and endpoint handshake provide strong but not fully behavioral authentication evidence.","The captured command text redacts the public-key blob, so its bytes cannot be independently compared with the task text; the exact-match assertion and displayed fingerprint are the available direct checks."]}
